    "abstract": "Our findings highlight the dominant role of rising atmospheric CO<sub>2</sub> in enhancing assimilation rate and increasing iWUE of Chinese pine and will aid in defining the performance of this tree species under climate change in northern China.",
    "field": "Forest ecophysiology",
    "problem": "The paper addresses the challenge of identifying the dominant drivers of increased tree intrinsic water use efficiency, as the relative contributions of rising atmospheric CO\u2082, vapor pressure deficit, and soil moisture, and their underlying physiological mechanisms, are not fully understood.",
    "method": "The authors developed tree-ring width, stable carbon, and oxygen isotopic chronologies from Chinese pine stands across northern China to quantify the ecophysiological responses of intrinsic water use efficiency (iWUE) to rising atmospheric CO2 and drought.",
    "task": "The task is to quantify the relative effects of rising atmospheric CO2, vapor pressure deficit, and soil moisture on the increased intrinsic water use efficiency of Chinese pine, using tree-ring width and stable isotope chronologies from sites across northern China.",
